#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049125 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048297 :topic/math-academy-id 147 :topic/title "Multiplying Square Matrices"}] :topic/id #uuid "dcaddd67-a2eb-5a06-88ab-be47014dbd14" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087699 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087506 :question/id #uuid "4f4be86f-5a12-5fdf-b98e-2095b1faae8f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4499" :question/problem "Using the method of direct verification, determine whether the matrix $B$ is the inverse of $A,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We must check whether $AB = BA = I_2.$\n\nComputing $AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $BA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B = A^{-1}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087700 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087507 :question/id #uuid "7ab2814b-dc93-569b-bdd6-618880da6e2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4500" :question/problem "Using the method of direct verification, determine whether the matrix $B$ is the inverse of $A,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we  check whether $AB = BA = I_3.$\n\nComputing $AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $BA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B = A^{-1}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a Larger Matrix"}] :topic/math-academy-id 863 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048146 :topic/math-academy-id 153 :topic/title "The Determinant of a 3x3 Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049063 :topic/math-academy-id 2706 :topic/title "Linear Transformations of Points and Lines in the Plane"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548 :topic/math-academy-id 864 :topic/title "Inverses of 2x2 Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049779 :topic/math-academy-id 42 :topic/title "Matrices With Easy-to-Find Inverses"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050021 :topic/math-academy-id 3118 :topic/title "Symmetric Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050576 :topic/math-academy-id 1726 :topic/title "Elementary 2x2 Matrices"}] :topic/title "Introduction to the Inverse of a Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050783 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048867 :topic/math-academy-id 203 :topic/title "Simplifying Expressions Using Basic Trigonometric Identities"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489 :topic/math-academy-id 478 :topic/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :topic/id #uuid "66b05ba4-f638-5531-98d7-9a7817e5a7aa" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064225 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064093 :question/id #uuid "15473603-5bb5-5a20-8fc9-e4d7589ccb38" :question/math-academy-id "e-473" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}\\left(\\cot x + \\sin^2 x\\right)\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply out the parentheses and then use the identity $\\dfrac{1}{\\sin x } = \\csc x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064226 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064094 :question/id #uuid "68d2eb66-87ab-5182-9669-376bd1f70673" :question/math-academy-id "e-474" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x =  \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\dfrac{\\sin x }{\\cos x}  = \\tan x$ and $\\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} = \\sec x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\sec x\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a standard trigonometric integral. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064227 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064095 :question/id #uuid "b34db891-0676-505c-9ba3-57d86f1d9b94" :question/math-academy-id "e-475" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\tan{2x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\tan 2x = \\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos 2x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan{2x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\int \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{\\cos 2x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNotice that the numerator of the integrand is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we can solve this by substitution.\n\nLet $u=\\cos 2x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=-2 \\sin 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\textrm d u= \\sin 2x \\, \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{-1}|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Trigonometric Expression Using Identities and Substitution"}] :topic/math-academy-id 282 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048717 :topic/math-academy-id 1037 :topic/title "Integration Using the Pythagorean Identities"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049850 :topic/math-academy-id 3224 :topic/title "Integrating Products of Trigonometric Functions"}] :topic/title "Integration Using Basic Trigonometric Identities"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048252 :topic/math-academy-id 862 :topic/title "Zero, Square, Diagonal and Identity Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954 :topic/math-academy-id 177 :topic/title "Calculating the Dot Product Using Components"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050979 :topic/math-academy-id 146 :topic/title "Scalar Multiplication of Matrices"}] :topic/id #uuid "69b57f07-ad86-5963-8f81-f63cb01f239f" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100866 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100688 :question/id #uuid "f31d614e-a00c-5502-8b23-e9214bc2ae5c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4397" :question/problem "Find $A\\mathbf{x}$ if \n![](images/cd/cd3816d9938d4bd65d0eaa5abcb3db329aed3827219a8cea0186ced2d5759880.png)\nand\n![](images/b9/b9c909115c604eb1a96463bdd31a71e77caa0a11d0098a8144afd798b50ae236.png)" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 5 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ 7\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](images/6d/6d470181fba760868835a72c5d5cfec3c90f9bf7bf7a9d86860dc8d981517652.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a 2x2 Matrix by a Column Vector"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100867 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100689 :question/id #uuid "63f6d2d5-0654-569f-bd71-437c835a49d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-4398" :question/problem "Find $A\\mathbf{x}$ if \n![](images/1c/1c38a037a66a5f86fd38a2b9ea6226420e455d5ef66fe776af7548fb6724841f.png)\nand\n![](images/ff/ffec9e0757e75cad35bbec65fb15d07eb580274c8beb4a9b3337d31cf964ec1c.png)" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 0 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3-2+0 \\\\ 2+0+0 \\\\ -1+4+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a 3x3 Matrix by a Column Vector"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100868 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100690 :question/id #uuid "c154ead3-0974-5e0d-9653-38c20756921e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4399" :question/problem "Find $A\\mathbf{x}$ if \n![](images/52/528d30567626c47a051ea97c39cfe64a6bb4538d54b8a355d004457eca98d106.png)\nand\n![](images/e7/e7fe9e8a74f76dfec4fd92313fe84650f6006e3c54795fb06e3a255e4dbe8296.png)" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}a & c \\\\ b & d\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}a\\cdot 3+c\\cdot 2 \\\\ b\\cdot 3+d\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3a+2c \\\\ 3b+2d\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Matrix With Variable Entries by a Column Vector"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1195 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048203 :topic/math-academy-id 1959 :topic/title "The Standard Matrix of a Linear Transformation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048783 :topic/math-academy-id 1198 :topic/title "Conformability for Matrix Multiplication"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049008 :topic/math-academy-id 1171 :topic/title "Multiplying a Matrix by the Identity Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049585 :topic/math-academy-id 1911 :topic/title "Markov Chains"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050008 :topic/math-academy-id 1928 :topic/title "The Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050463 :topic/math-academy-id 1729 :topic/title "Representing 2x2 Systems of Equations Using a Matrix Product"}] :topic/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column Vector"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050898 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332 :topic/math-academy-id 1159 :topic/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution"}] :topic/id #uuid "6f6a1e18-10c1-5fb2-9f41-d2b49cf97ce7" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100816 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100608 :question/id #uuid "8ea17e06-1248-510c-9e71-c57da0cb0d02" :question/math-academy-id "e-14405" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{3}{x \\sqrt{\\ln x}} \\, \\textrm{d}x }.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $u = \\ln x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac 1 x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}u=\\dfrac 1 x\\,{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{u}+C \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Functions Containing the Natural Logarithm"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100817 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100609 :question/id #uuid "1579f932-6e7f-5daa-b001-54a9f6c236ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-14406" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{\\log^2_3 x }{x} \\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{(\\log_3 x)^2 }{x} \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\log_3 x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln 3}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\ln 3\\,\\textrm d u = \\dfrac 1 x \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\int {u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3({log}_{3}⁡x)^{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3{log}_{3}^{3}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Functions Containing Other Logarithms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100818 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100610 :question/id #uuid "d2f49acf-ed91-510d-a0f1-41c50eeef9e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-14422" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int_{e}^{e^2} \\dfrac{\\ln^2 x}{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $u = \\ln x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac 1 x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}u=\\dfrac 1 x\\,{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $e$ | $e^2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\int_{e}^{e^2} \\dfrac{\\ln^2 x}{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n&= \\int_{e}^{e^2} (\\ln x)^2\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x}\\[5pt]\n&=\\int_{1}^{2} u^2\\,\\textrm{d}u\\[5pt]\n&=\\left.\\dfrac13u^{3}\\right|_{1}^{2}\\[5pt] \n&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(2^3-1^3 \\right)\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(8-1 \\right)\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{7}{3}\t\t\n\n\t}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Definite Integrals"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1161 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050207 :topic/math-academy-id 1992 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Rectangular Domains"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050331 :topic/math-academy-id 2100 :topic/title "Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :topic/title "Integrating Logarithmic Functions Using Substitution"}]}
